  {
    "id": "hira-dakuten",
    "day": 6,
    "title": "Voiced sounds: ゛ and ゜",
    "explanation": "Two small marks change a kana's sound. Two short strokes (゛, called dakuten) give the consonant a heavier, buzzing sound: k → g (か → が), s → z (さ → ざ), t → d (た → だ) and h → b (は → ば). A small circle (゜, called handakuten) goes only on the は row and turns h into p: は → ぱ. ぢ and づ sound just like じ (ji) and ず (zu) and appear in only a few words, such as つづく (to continue), so when you hear 'ji' or 'zu' it is almost always じ or ず.",
