  {
    "id": "hira-yoon",
    "day": 7,
    "title": "Small ゃ ゅ ょ, small っ and long vowels",
    "explanation": "A small ゃ, ゅ or ょ after an i-sound kana (き, し, ち, に, ひ, み, り and their ゛ ゜ forms) blends the two into one beat: き + ゃ = きゃ (kya), while a full-size きや is two beats, ki-ya. A small っ is a one-beat pause that doubles the next consonant: おと oto (sound) vs おっと otto (husband). A long vowel lasts two beats and is written with an extra vowel: おばさん (aunt) vs おばあさん (grandmother). A long 'e' is usually spelled えい (せんせい) and a long 'o' usually おう (ありがとう); ええ and おお appear only in a few words, such as おねえさん and おおきい.",
