  {
    "id": "kata-extended",
    "day": 14,
    "title": "Extended sounds for loanwords",
    "explanation": "Loanwords need some sounds that the basic kana can't spell, so katakana builds them by adding a small ァ, ィ, ゥ, ェ or ォ (or ュ, as in デュ) to a normal kana: テ + ィ = ティ (ti), フ + ァ = ファ (fa), ウ + ィ = ウィ (wi), チ + ェ = チェ (che). Each combination is said as one beat, just like キャ. ヴ (ウ with ゛) spells a 'v', but most people say it like 'b', and many words are more often written with the バ row: バイオリン is more common than ヴァイオリン for 'violin'.",
